@startuml Emergency Stop and Breakdown Bay

title Emergency Stop / Breakdown Bay (Nothaltebucht) vs Hard Shoulder (Standstreifen)

legend
    | **Feature**      | **Standstreifen**             | **Nothaltebucht**                      |
    | Shape            | Continuous strip              | Recessed alcoves at intervals          |
    | Location         | Open motorways                | Tunnels / roads without shoulders      |
    | Availability     | Anywhere along the road       | Only at specific points                |
    | Purpose          | Emergency stopping            | Emergency stopping where no shoulder exists |
end legend

start

partition "Behaviour when Breaking Down near a Nothaltebucht" {
    :Vehicle breaks down;
    if (Can you keep rolling?) then (yes)
        :Steer towards the nearest **Nothaltebucht**;
        note right
            Do NOT stop in the driving lane
            or tunnel if you can reach the bay.
        end note
    else (no)
        :Stop as far right as possible;
    endif

    :Turn on **hazard lights**;
    :Switch off the **engine**;
    note right
        Especially important in tunnels
        to prevent fume buildup.
    end note
    :Exit the vehicle and stay in\nthe bay away from traffic;

    if (Is there fire or serious danger?) then (yes)
        :Follow **emergency exit signs** (Notausgang)\ndo not stay with the vehicle;
        stop
    else (no)
        :Call for help via nearby\n**emergency phone (Notrufsäule)**\nor dial **112**;
        note right
            Emergency phones are usually
            located near breakdown bays.
        end note
        :Do NOT attempt repairs on the carriageway;
        stop
    endif
}

@enduml
